<!-- Declares the WiFi HAL. Ships in the cycle AFTER init.arcfox-wifi.rc, on
     purpose.

     The rule on this device is: never declare a HAL in VINTF until its service is
     PROVEN to reach registration. Declaring one that cannot register wedges its
     client in an untimed waitForDeclaredService and has cost four boots here
     (weaver, fingerprint, NFC, and a premature sensors declaration).

     bc25 supplied that proof. With only the init rc in place, the service starts,
     initialises, and dies at exactly one point:

       I android.hardware.wifi-service: Wifi Hal is booting up...
       F android.hardware.wifi-service: service.cpp:64] Check failed:
           result == STATUS_OK (result=-3, STATUS_OK=0) Failed to register wifi HAL

     -3 is EX_ILLEGAL_ARGUMENT from AServiceManager_addService via
     meetsDeclarationRequirements(): an "android.hardware." name that is not
     VINTF-declared cannot register. Nothing else is wrong; it got past
     "booting up", i.e. past HAL init, and the missing declaration is the only
     thing rejecting it. Same sequence that took sensors from a boot-killing crash
     loop to 68 working sensors (bc22 fix crash -> reaches addService -> bc23
     declare -> registers).

     Version 4 is read off the binary, not assumed: android.hardware.wifi-service
     links android.hardware.wifi-V4-ndk.so, and that library is present in
     /vendor/lib64. The instance name is service.cpp:57,
     Wifi::descriptor + "/default".

     Filename deliberately differs from any AOSP-shipped fragment name so kati
     cannot fail with "already defined by", the same reason
     android.hardware.sensors-arcfox.xml is named as it is.

     The kernel side is already up: qca_cld3_kiwi_v2, cnss2, cfg80211, mac80211
     and rmnet_wlan are all loaded. There is simply no wlan0 yet, because nothing
     had ever called into the HAL to create one. -->
<manifest version="1.0" type="device">
    <hal format="aidl">
        <name>android.hardware.wifi</name>
        <version>4</version>
        <fqname>IWifi/default</fqname>
    </hal>
</manifest>
